// THE MUNICIPAL BUILD WORKFLOW, SIMULATED ACROSS SIX TOWNS (operator request
// 2026-08-30: a gradient anchored on the hometown - Washington NJ 07882 - out to
// Jersey City). This is the OWN-SPACE rung's inquiry instrument made concrete: the
// same questions the colo RFQ asks of a rented supplier, asked of a utility and a
// parcel. Scope pinned to loads in the ordinary-request planning band (up to a few
// hundred kW) - what this program EXPECTS to be an ordinary commercial service
// request rather than a studied project; the utility classifies the request itself.
// Every duration/cost band here is a PLANNING ESTIMATE at directory grain; each is
// replaced by the receipt the workflow stage itself produces (a will-serve letter,
// a quote, a permit) - the stages are the instrument, this file only names them.
//
// WHAT THE DECLARED WORKFLOW COVERS TODAY IS NARROWER THAN THAT TITLE. Only the
// candidate-grain prefix - discovery, the regulatory census, and the market-area
// screening gate that consumes it - is declared, because every later step asks a
// question about a specific property that does not exist yet. The stopping point is
// a typed row, not a silence; see property_identity_needed.

// ---- planning bands: what WE expect from a load, not what the utility will call it ----

// THE TIERS WERE THIS PROGRAM'S OWN EXPECTATION WEARING THE UTILITY'S VOCABULARY. ServiceTier
// named three arms - SmallCommercial, StudiedDistribution, TransmissionScale - selected from
// requested kW alone by two thresholds nobody cited, under a heading that said difficulty is a
// function of requested kW AND NOTHING ELSE. That sentence is false and the type made it
// load-bearing: which process a utility applies to a service request is decided by the utility
// under its tariff, from the requested load together with the capacity actually present at the
// address, the condition of the circuit that serves it, and its own thresholds - none of which
// this program has observed for any address, because it has no address (see
// property_identity_needed).
//
// So the vocabulary is given back. What survives is what a planner can honestly compute with no
// address at all: a BAND, self-authored, used to decide how much runway to expect before asking
// anyone. A band is not a classification and cannot stand in for one - it has no authority
// behind it, and the type says so rather than the prose.
type ServicePlanningBand
  = OrdinaryRequestExpected
  | StudyExpected
  | MajorInterconnectExpected

// THE THRESHOLDS ARE WRITTEN ONCE. The first revision of this repair carried the interval on the
// band arms AND repeated it as literals in the selection - two authorities for one fact, which is
// the class this whole item exists to remove, reintroduced by the repair itself. They had already
// diverged: StudyExpected declared floor_kw 400 while 400 selects the ordinary band, and
// MajorInterconnectExpected declared floor_kw 5000 while 5000 selects study. The payload was
// describing an interval the function did not implement, and nothing could see the difference.
//
// So these two numbers are the authority and every bound is derived from them. They are this
// program's own convention, not a tariff fact: the load at which the surveyed utilities'
// published small-commercial process stopped being the obvious one, chosen to plan against. A
// cited tariff threshold would be a fact about a named utility and would live in extdeps beside
// that utility, not here.
// AND THE CONVENTION IS A VALUE, SO THAT CONSUMING IT IS OBSERVABLE. Two bare declarations were
// a single authority in the only sense a reader could check by eye: a consumer that spelled 400
// instead of ordinary_through_kw behaved identically, so no probe could tell the declaration from
// an equal literal, and a future edit could reintroduce the second authority at the same value
// without any wall firing. A convention that can be VARIED makes the difference observable - a
// witness supplies a different one and everything that genuinely reads it moves together.
type ServicePlanningConvention { ordinary_through_kw: Int, study_through_kw: Int }

data survey_planning_convention: ServicePlanningConvention = ServicePlanningConvention {
  ordinary_through_kw: 400,
  study_through_kw: 5000,
}

// A BAND RUNS FROM ABOVE THE PREVIOUS THRESHOLD THROUGH ITS OWN, and the names say which end is
// included, because "floor" and "ceiling" did not: the old floor_kw was one below the smallest
// load the band actually contains.
fn planning_band_for_kw(c: ServicePlanningConvention, kw: Int) -> ServicePlanningBand {
  if kw <= c.ordinary_through_kw { OrdinaryRequestExpected } else {
    if kw <= c.study_through_kw { StudyExpected } else { MajorInterconnectExpected }
  }
}

// The bounds are a PROJECTION of the same two numbers, never a second copy. An empty list is an
// open end - the ordinary band has no lower bound a load can fall below, and the major band has
// no upper one - rather than a sentinel standing in for one.
type BandBounds { above_kw: List<Int>, through_kw: List<Int> }

fn band_bounds(c: ServicePlanningConvention, b: ServicePlanningBand) -> BandBounds {
  match b {
    OrdinaryRequestExpected => BandBounds { above_kw: [], through_kw: [c.ordinary_through_kw] },
    StudyExpected => BandBounds { above_kw: [c.ordinary_through_kw], through_kw: [c.study_through_kw] },
    MajorInterconnectExpected => BandBounds { above_kw: [c.study_through_kw], through_kw: [] },
  }
}

// AND THE AUTHORITATIVE ANSWER IS AN OBSERVATION THIS PROGRAM OWES, not a value it may derive.
// The utility's own classification of a service request is an external fact about a named
// address, so it enters through the same door every other external fact does - a receipt on a
// stage, produced by the utility as an external subject. It cannot be declared yet for the reason
// every other property-specific row was deleted: there is no parcel identity to name a service
// address with. The obligation is recorded rather than the row invented.
data utility_service_classification_needed: WorkflowExtensionObligation = WorkflowExtensionObligation {
  self_ref: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "strategy.site_power_survey",
    decl_name: "utility_service_classification_needed",
    field: WholeDeclaration,
  },
  after_node: gate_market_area_screening.self_ref,
  needs_grain: ParcelGrain,
  because: "the utility's classification of a service request is its own decision under its tariff, made about a specific service address against the capacity present there; this program can band a load but cannot classify a service, and has no address to ask about",
}

// ---- sizing: what a service rating buys in Mt. Collins nodes ----

// A service is sized at 125 percent of continuous load (the same NEC continuous
// rule the circuit menu applies), so usable continuous watts = rating x 0.8.
fn continuous_watts_of_service(service_kw: Int) -> Int {
  service_kw * 1000 * 800 / 1000
}

fn collins_nodes_at_service(service_kw: Int) -> Int {
  continuous_watts_of_service(service_kw: service_kw) / collins_node_planning_watts
}

// 13 nodes per 208V/30A three-phase feed (rack_power_engineering nodes_per_circuit).
fn racks_at_service(service_kw: Int) -> Int {
  let n = collins_nodes_at_service(service_kw: service_kw)
  (n + 12) / 13
}

// ---- the declarable workflow: everything the CANDIDATE grain can carry, and nothing past it ----

// WHAT THIS REVISION DELETES, AND WHY. The previous revision declared the whole dirt-up workflow
// - route selection, site control, site-plan approval, construction, as-built conformance,
// inspection, energize - against site_washington, a market-area SiteCandidate. Every one of
// those rows asks a PROPERTY-SPECIFIC question: a will-serve letter is for a service address, a
// deed or lease is for a parcel, a site-plan approval and an electrical cut-in are decisions
// about a building. Declaring them at candidate grain did not model them; it relabelled them,
// and the relabelling was load-bearing, because those requirements then closed gates.
//
// The same edit removes a jurisdiction fault of the same kind: those rows named
// phillipsburg_zoning_officer as the issuing authority for a WASHINGTON candidate. An authority
// we have not identified is an open obligation, and filling it with an exact officer from a
// different municipality is the two-values-that-never-meet defect at its most concrete.
//
// Inventing a parcel identity to make the rows writable again would repeat the error one layer
// down, so the workflow ENDS where the identity ends, and says so in a typed row rather than in
// prose. What is deleted here is not the modelling - the substrate keeps every route, activation
// and conditional-edge capability, exercised by the module witness - it is the CLAIM that this
// program can currently describe work it cannot name a subject for.

fn here(name: NonEmptyStr) -> DeclarationRef {
  DeclarationRef { module_path: "strategy.site_power_survey", decl_name: name, field: WholeDeclaration }
}

data ref_candidate: DeclarationRef = here(name: "site_washington")

// THE REVISION IS AN IDENTITY, NOT A SENTENCE. It was a prose NonEmptyStr doing two jobs at once
// - naming which design a document answers for, and describing it - so a reworded description
// silently invalidated every receipt while a materially changed design that kept its wording did
// not. The facts that make one revision different from another are now fields, and the join is
// declaration identity.
type SiteUseRevision {
  self_ref: DeclarationRef
  fleet_plan: DeclarationRef
  requested_service_kw: Int
  building_posture: NonEmptyStr
  generator_posture: NonEmptyStr
  fuel_storage_posture: NonEmptyStr
}

data use_revision_a: SiteUseRevision = SiteUseRevision {
  self_ref: here(name: "use_revision_a"),
  fleet_plan: DeclarationRef { module_path: "strategy.rack_power_engineering", decl_name: "collins_fleet_nodes", field: WholeDeclaration },
  requested_service_kw: 200,
  building_posture: "compute room inside a host building; no dedicated structure",
  generator_posture: "none",
  fuel_storage_posture: "none",
}

fn ordering(pred: DeclarationRef) -> WorkflowDependency {
  WorkflowDependency { predecessor: pred, required_when: AlwaysActive, kind: OrderingOnly }
}

// ---- the candidate-grain prefix ----

data stage_site_inquiry: WorkStage = WorkStage {
  self_ref: here(name: "stage_site_inquiry"),
  title: "market-area and candidate discovery",
  depends_on: [],
  planning_owner: "us + commercial broker",
  planning_duration_band: "weeks to months; an existing light-industrial building is preferred because it collapses the zoning question to near-nothing",
}

data stage_regulatory_diligence: WorkStage = WorkStage {
  self_ref: here(name: "stage_regulatory_diligence"),
  title: "regulatory census for the market area",
  depends_on: [ordering(pred: stage_site_inquiry.self_ref)],
  planning_owner: "us + land-use attorney for anything ambiguous",
  planning_duration_band: "days of reading per town; a measure adopted during closing is the failure this work exists to catch",
}

data req_regulatory_census: EvidenceRequirement = EvidenceRequirement {
  self_ref: here(name: "req_regulatory_census"),
  gate: here(name: "gate_market_area_screening"),
  producer: ProducedByStage { stage: stage_regulatory_diligence.self_ref },
  subject: AboutCandidate { candidate: ref_candidate },
  design_revision: use_revision_a.self_ref,
  applies_to: AnyRoute,
  describes: "the per-layer regulatory census for this market area, every instrument touching the use at this revision, plus a pending-measure scan",
}

// THIS GATE ASKS A QUESTION THE CANDIDATE GRAIN CAN ANSWER: is this market area worth naming a
// property in? It does not ask whether any particular property is authorized - that question has
// no subject until a property exists.
data gate_market_area_screening: DecisionGate = DecisionGate {
  self_ref: here(name: "gate_market_area_screening"),
  title: "does this market area survive the regulatory census at this use revision?",
  purpose: Verification { measures: "every instrument the census found, read against this use revision, with each open question named rather than assumed absent" },
  depends_on: [ordering(pred: stage_regulatory_diligence.self_ref)],
  requirements: [req_regulatory_census],
}

// ---- where the workflow stops, and what would let it continue ----

data property_identity_needed: WorkflowExtensionObligation = WorkflowExtensionObligation {
  self_ref: here(name: "property_identity_needed"),
  after_node: gate_market_area_screening.self_ref,
  needs_grain: ParcelGrain,
  because: "every remaining step - will-serve, commitment terms, route selection, site control, site-plan approval, construction conformance, inspection and energize - is a question about a specific property and its issuing authorities. Neither exists yet, and a candidate-grain subject beside an authority from another municipality is a relabelling, not a model",
}

data municipal_build_template: WorkflowTemplate = WorkflowTemplate {
  selection: NoSelection,
  specs: [
    WorkflowNodeSpec { node: StageNode { stage: stage_site_inquiry }, activation: AlwaysActive },
    WorkflowNodeSpec { node: StageNode { stage: stage_regulatory_diligence }, activation: AlwaysActive },
    WorkflowNodeSpec { node: GateNode { gate: gate_market_area_screening }, activation: AlwaysActive },
  ],
}

data municipal_template_admission: WorkflowGraphAdmission = admit_template(t: municipal_build_template)

// NO RECEIPTS AND NO DECISION EXIST. The census has not been produced, so the screening gate
// stands pending - which is the correct reading of a program that has surveyed towns and
// committed to none.
data municipal_build_receipts: List<EvidenceReceipt> = []
data municipal_selection_decisions: List<SelectionDecision> = []

// ---- the six candidate sites ----

type SiteUrbanity = SmallTown | RiverCityPostIndustrial | SuburbanCommercial | UrbanCore

// THE MONOTONE LAYER RULE IS DELETED, INCLUDING HERE. It was removed from
// extdeps.regulation.types as unsound - authority is a function of subject and scope, not
// altitude - and this consumer went on restating it, which is the deleted rule surviving in the
// place it is actually read. Instruments now relate through the typed AuthorityRelation graph;
// there is no layer ordering left to appeal to.
//
// Facts we have not read stay OPEN QUESTIONS on the site row - a coverage obligation here in
// the survey, never fabricated rows inside a jurisdiction's extdeps module.
type DiligenceStanding
  = DiligenceOpen { questions: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | DiligenceCleared { census_receipt: NonEmptyStr }

type RegulatoryDiligence {
  standing: DiligenceStanding
  findings: List<RegulatoryInstrumentFinding>
}

fn diligence_findings(d: RegulatoryDiligence) -> List<RegulatoryInstrumentFinding> {
  d.findings
}

fn diligence_open_questions(d: RegulatoryDiligence) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match d.standing {
    DiligenceOpen { questions } => questions,
    DiligenceCleared { census_receipt } => [],
  }
}

// EVERY LEGAL-FORCE QUESTION IS ASKED AS OF A DATE. Without one, an instrument effective from a
// future date reads as presently binding - the defect review of PR #24 found in the first
// revision, which asked the question with no as-of at all.
data survey_as_of: EpochMs = 1788091200000

// THE USE THIS SURVEY IS ASKING ABOUT - a 21-node Mt. Collins fleet at 650W planning wall draw
// per node, in a compute room inside a host building, with no generator and no fuel storage.
// A determination is admitted only against a stated use profile and revision, and the revision is
// load-bearing: change the load, footprint or programme and a prior ruling stops governing. Those
// three facts were carried as query fields and as two data rows, and NOTHING READ ANY OF THEM -
// mutating them changed no admission, effect or clearance. They are removed rather than kept as
// decoration, and return with DU-0 item 4's authority receipt, which is the first consumer that
// can compare a determination's scope against the use it was issued for.
fn market_area_query(instrument: DeclarationRef) -> ApplicabilityQuery {
  ApplicabilityQuery { instrument: instrument }
}

data standard_dc_diligence_questions: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "municipal: any data-center-specific ordinance, ban, or moratorium, enacted or pending - read from the MUNICIPAL CODE, never inferred from absence on a statewide roster (Phillipsburg is adopted-and-absent, the executed counterexample)?",
  "municipal: does this town's OWN data-center definition reach a compute room inside a building used mainly for something else? Asked per instrument on each finding rather than in one shared wording - the three NJ instruments modeled so far use materially different definitional tests, and a question that misquotes the ordinance it asks about would be carried to a zoning officer as if it were their text.",
  "municipal: which zones permit the use by-right vs conditionally, under the state land-use law?",
  "municipal/county: noise and generator/fuel-storage rules at the parcel's lot lines?",
  "state: utility/BPU or PUC measures touching large new loads or DC tariffs?",
  "state/federal: environmental overlays on the parcel (brownfield status, floodplain, air permits for gensets)?",
]

type SiteCandidate {
  town: NonEmptyStr
  state: NonEmptyStr
  utility: ElectricUtility
  urbanity: SiteUrbanity
  posture: NonEmptyStr
  diligence: RegulatoryDiligence
}

data site_washington: SiteCandidate = SiteCandidate {
  town: "Washington (07882)", state: "NJ", utility: jcpl, urbanity: SmallTown,
  posture: "the hometown anchor: Warren County small borough, cheap light-industrial stock, thin fiber - carrier options are the question to survey before power, since JCP&L will serve trivially at this tier",
  diligence: RegulatoryDiligence { standing: DiligenceOpen { questions: standard_dc_diligence_questions }, findings: [] },
}

data site_easton: SiteCandidate = SiteCandidate {
  town: "Easton", state: "PA", utility: met_ed, urbanity: RiverCityPostIndustrial,
  posture: "Lehigh Valley river city: post-industrial building stock, PA commercial power typically cheaper than NJ, same FirstEnergy parent as JCP&L but under the PA PUC - the two-state arbitrage candidate",
  diligence: RegulatoryDiligence { standing: DiligenceOpen { questions: standard_dc_diligence_questions }, findings: [] },
}

// THE PAIRED-COMPARISON CLAIM IS WITHDRAWN, AND THE MECHANISM THAT WITHDREW IT IS THE FINDING.
// This row was authored to "isolate the STATE variable with geography held fixed" against
// Easton. It cannot: Phillipsburg adopted Ordinance 2026-08 on 2026-04-14 prohibiting data
// centers in every zoning district - four months before this survey named it - and a municipal
// prohibition IS a state-regime effect, so the variable the pair was meant to isolate is what
// removed the row from comparison. The pair survives as a MATCHED REGIONAL PAIR (analyst ruling
// 2026-08-30): utility, parcel, building stock, tax, flood and carrier conditions still differ
// across the river, so a contrast may be projected after controlling the evidence actually
// held - it may never be reported as causal isolation inferred from geographic proximity.
// DERIVED FROM THE INSTRUMENT'S OWN LIFECYCLE, NEVER AUTHORED HERE. A hand-set standing on
// the consuming row is a second authority for a fact the instrument already carries, and
// it would go stale silently the day the ordinance's row changes.
data site_phillipsburg: SiteCandidate = SiteCandidate {
  town: "Phillipsburg", state: "NJ", utility: jcpl, urbanity: RiverCityPostIndustrial,
  posture: "Easton's NJ twin across the Delaware: same building-stock character, NJ regime. Carried as a matched regional comparator and as the census's live example of a prohibiting instrument on a candidate - NOT as a selectable market area while Ordinance 2026-08 stands unread and undetermined.",
  diligence: RegulatoryDiligence {
    standing: DiligenceOpen { questions: standard_dc_diligence_questions },
    findings: [RegulatoryInstrumentFinding {
      instrument: phillipsburg_ordinance_o2026_08,
      remaining_obligations: [
        "Transcribe or independently verify the ADOPTED text and read its temporal / savings provisions. The adopted four-page instrument IS pinned as this row's provenance; what is missing is machine-readable content, because that PDF is a CCITT fax scan carrying no text layer. The earlier wording here said the digested copy was the introduced version, which stopped being true when the adopted scan was pinned.",
        "Retrieve the publication notice fixing the effective date under Section 7.",
      ],
    }],
  },
}

data site_hackettstown: SiteCandidate = SiteCandidate {
  town: "Hackettstown", state: "NJ", utility: jcpl, urbanity: SmallTown,
  posture: "Warren County town with a real industrial base (the Mars plant anchors utility-scale commercial service familiarity); slightly better transport links than Washington",
  diligence: RegulatoryDiligence { standing: DiligenceOpen { questions: standard_dc_diligence_questions }, findings: [] },
}

data site_morristown: SiteCandidate = SiteCandidate {
  town: "Morristown", state: "NJ", utility: jcpl, urbanity: SuburbanCommercial,
  posture: "the slightly-urban probe: Morris County HQ market - land and rent price OUT the small-datacenter use well before power does; expect zoning friction and no industrial stock",
  diligence: RegulatoryDiligence { standing: DiligenceOpen { questions: standard_dc_diligence_questions }, findings: [] },
}

// JERSEY CITY IS NOT A CITYWIDE BAN, AND CALLING IT ONE WAS THE ERROR. An earlier revision
// carried no instrument here at all, on the stated grounds that designation, dates, extent and
// definition were all unread - and the row was reported up the lane as "BANNED" anyway. Both
// halves were wrong: the ordinance is published, and it is a NAMED-DISTRICT instrument. Ord.
// 26-057 strikes cyber hotels/data centers from the permitted principal uses of the I Industrial
// District alone; every other district is untouched, and offices remain permitted in Industrial.
//
// This is the row that shows why extent had to be modeled rather than reduced to a Boolean. It
// is also, on the city's own account of its existing downtown stock, the market area where the
// accessory-use question is most clearly OPEN - the opposite of what "banned" conveys.
data site_jersey_city: SiteCandidate = SiteCandidate {
  town: "Jersey City", state: "NJ", utility: pseg, urbanity: UrbanCore,
  posture: "the super-urban probe: PSE&G territory, richest fiber/interconnection in the state (the carrier-hotel corridor), but industrial land is scarce and contested and the grid is loaded. The row was authored expecting POWER to be the binding constraint here; the regulatory answer arrived first.",
  diligence: RegulatoryDiligence {
    standing: DiligenceOpen { questions: standard_dc_diligence_questions },
    findings: [RegulatoryInstrumentFinding {
      instrument: jc_ordinance_26_057,
      remaining_obligations: [
        "Retrieve the publication receipt fixing the effective date. The adoption receipt is NOT outstanding: the digested ordinance records the 2026-08-19 final 9-0 vote and the Mayor's 2026-08-20 approval on its own certification page.",
        "Because the extent is a single district, the parcel search here is not foreclosed - obtain the zoning-map district for any candidate parcel before ranking it.",
      ],
    }],
  },
}



// No municipal-electric shortcut exists on this list: all six are investor-owned
// territory (JCP&L x4, Met-Ed, PSE&G). NJ's municipal electrics (Madison, Butler,
// et al.) are elsewhere - a deliberate future widening of the census if the
// same-government permitting advantage proves decisive.
data site_candidates: List<SiteCandidate> = [
  site_washington, site_easton, site_phillipsburg,
  site_hackettstown, site_morristown, site_jersey_city,
]
